 ## Overview
-Weight regularization is a loss term applied to neural radiance fields (NeRFs) to suppress geometric noise and cloud-like semi-transparent artifacts that arise when training alters density distributions [S1]. It encourages a concentrated density distribution [S1]. A related strategy forces rays to either have zero weights along the entire ray or consolidate all weights into as small an area as possible [S2, unofficial]. Unlike mip-NeRF 360, which optimizes distances to reduce floaters, this method explicitly penalizes weight scattering to maintain clean geometry during optimization [S1].
+Weight regularization is a loss term applied to neural radiance fields (NeRFs) to suppress geometric noise and cloud-like semi-transparent artifacts that arise when training alters density distributions [S1]. It encourages a concentrated density distribution [S1]. A related strategy forces rays to either have zero weights along the entire ray or consolidate all weights into as small an area as possible [S2, unofficial]. Unlike mip-NeRF 360, which optimizes distances to reduce floaters, this method explicitly penalizes weight scattering to maintain clean geometry during optimization [S1]. It builds on [Distortion Loss with Power Transformation](4560-distortion-loss-with-power-transformation.md) and differs from [Gradient Scaling](6887-gradient-scaling.md) by targeting weight distribution directly rather than gradient magnitudes.
 
